**Answer: No.** Such a partition does not always exist. The infimum of upper sums need not be attained by any partition.

## Counterexample

Let $A = [0,1]$ and $f(x) = x$, which is bounded and continuous on $[0,1]$.

For any partition $P = \{0 = x_0 < x_1 < \cdots < x_n = 1\}$, since $f$ is increasing, the supremum on each subinterval $[x_{i-1}, x_i]$ is $M_i = x_i$. Thus

$$U(f, P) = \sum_{i=1}^{n} x_i\,(x_i - x_{i-1}).$$

Likewise $m_i = x_{i-1}$, so

$$L(f, P) = \sum_{i=1}^{n} x_{i-1}\,(x_i - x_{i-1}).$$

**Computing the integral.** Since $f(x)=x$ is continuous, it is Riemann integrable, and

$$\int_0^1 x\,dx = \sum_{i=1}^{n} \int_{x_{i-1}}^{x_i} x\,dx = \sum_{i=1}^{n} \frac{x_i^2 - x_{i-1}^2}{2} = \sum_{i=1}^{n} \frac{(x_i + x_{i-1})(x_i - x_{i-1})}{2} = \frac{U(f,P) + L(f,P)}{2} = \frac{1}{2}.$$

**The gap is strictly positive.** The difference between the upper and lower sums is

$$U(f, P) - L(f, P) = \sum_{i=1}^{n} (x_i - x_{i-1})(x_i - x_{i-1}) = \sum_{i=1}^{n} (\Delta x_i)^2.$$

Every partition of $[0,1]$ has at least one subinterval with positive length, so $\sum (\Delta x_i)^2 > 0$. Therefore

$$U(f, P) = \frac{1}{2} + \frac{1}{2}\sum_{i=1}^{n}(\Delta x_i)^2 > \frac{1}{2} \quad \text{for every partition } P.$$

**The infimum.** Since $f$ is Riemann integrable, $U(f) = \inf_P U(f,P) = \int_0^1 x\,dx = \tfrac{1}{2}$. (Refining partitions drives the mesh to $0$, and $\sum (\Delta x_i)^2 \le (\max \Delta x_i)\sum \Delta x_i = \max \Delta x_i \to 0$.)

**Conclusion.** We have $U(f) = \tfrac{1}{2}$, yet $U(f,P) > \tfrac{1}{2}$ for **every** partition $P$. Hence there is no partition $P$ with $U(f,P) = U(f)$.

## General Principle

The quantity $U(f) = \inf\{U(f,P) : P \in \mathcal{P}\}$ is an infimum over an infinite set. An infimum is attained only when the set has a minimum. For any non-constant continuous (or more generally, any Riemann-integrable but not "upper-step" — i.e., not constant on every subinterval of some partition) function, the upper sum strictly exceeds the integral on every partition, so the infimum is never achieved. The infimum is approached as a limit under refinement but is not realized by any single partition.

### PROOF COMPLETE
